      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] **Verify Error-Path `close()` Calls Precisely \u0026 Cover the `timeout_ms \u003d\u003d 0` Boundary:**\n\n1. **Error-path `close()` count masked by `~RtsUsbDevice()`:** Because `~RtsUsbDevice()` also calls `release()` -\u003e `backend_-\u003eclose()` when `device_` is destroyed at teardown, `init()` on failure triggers **3** `close()` calls in total (once before `backend_-\u003einit()`, once on the `open()` failure path, and once in `~RtsUsbDevice()`). Using `Times(::testing::AtLeast(2))` in `InitReleasesWhenDeviceNotFound` (line 70) would still pass even if the failure-path `release()` in `init()` were deleted (since entry `release()` + destructor `release()` \u003d 2). Using `InSequence` and expecting the exact call sequence ensures both `release()` calls in `init()` and `wait_for_device()` are verified (and similarly in `ReleaseClosesBackend` on line 202, expecting `Times(2)` distinguishes `device_-\u003erelease()` from the destructor call).\n2. **`timeout_ms \u003d\u003d 0` boundary in `WaitForDeviceRejectsInvalidIntervals`:** Add `timeout_ms \u003d\u003d 0` alongside `-1` and verify `backend_-\u003einit()` is never called:\n\n```cpp\nTEST_F(RtsUsbDeviceTest, InitReleasesWhenDeviceNotFound)\n{\n\tInSequence seq;\n\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, close());\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, init()).WillOnce(Return(0));\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, open(kVid, kPid)).WillOnce(Return(false));\n\t/* Once on the failure path and once in ~RtsUsbDevice(). */\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, close()).Times(2);\n\n\tEXPECT_FALSE(device_-\u003einit(kVid, kPid));\n}\n\nTEST_F(RtsUsbDeviceTest, InitReleasesPreviousResourcesFirst)\n{\n\tInSequence seq;\n\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, close());\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, init()).WillOnce(Return(0));\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, open(kVid, kPid)).WillOnce(Return(true));\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, close());\n\n\tEXPECT_TRUE(device_-\u003einit(kVid, kPid));\n}\n\nTEST_F(RtsUsbDeviceTest, WaitForDeviceOpensImmediately)\n{\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, init()).WillOnce(Return(0));\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, open(kVid, kPid)).WillOnce(Return(true));\n\n\tEXPECT_TRUE(\n\t\tdevice_-\u003ewait_for_device(kVid, kPid, kTimeoutMs, kPollIntervalMs));\n\tEXPECT_TRUE(slept_.empty());\n}\n\nTEST_F(RtsUsbDeviceTest, WaitForDevicePollsUntilDeviceAppears)\n{\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, init()).WillOnce(Return(0));\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, open(kVid, kPid))\n\t\t.WillOnce(Return(false))\n\t\t.WillOnce(Return(false))\n\t\t.WillOnce(Return(true));\n\n\tEXPECT_TRUE(\n\t\tdevice_-\u003ewait_for_device(kVid, kPid, kTimeoutMs, kPollIntervalMs));\n\tEXPECT_EQ(slept_,\n\t\t  (std::vector\u003cint\u003e{ kPollIntervalMs, kPollIntervalMs }));\n}\n\nTEST_F(RtsUsbDeviceTest, WaitForDeviceTimesOut)\n{\n\tInSequence seq;\n\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, close());\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, init()).WillOnce(Return(0));\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, open(kVid, kPid))\n\t\t.Times(3)\n\t\t.WillRepeatedly(Return(false));\n\t/* Once on the timeout path and once in ~RtsUsbDevice(). */\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, close()).Times(2);\n\n\tEXPECT_FALSE(device_-\u003ewait_for_device(\n\t\tkVid, kPid, 3 * kPollIntervalMs, kPollIntervalMs));\n\tEXPECT_EQ(slept_,\n\t\t  (std::vector\u003cint\u003e{ kPollIntervalMs, kPollIntervalMs,\n\t\t\t\t     kPollIntervalMs }));\n}\n\nTEST_F(RtsUsbDeviceTest, WaitForDeviceRejectsInvalidIntervals)\n{\n\tEXPECT_CALL(*backend_, init()).Times(0);\n\n\tEXPECT_FALSE(device_-\u003ewait_for_device(kVid, kPid, kTimeoutMs, 0));\n\tEXPECT_FALSE(device_-\u003ewait_for_device(kVid, kPid, kTimeoutMs, -1));\n\tEXPECT_FALSE(device_-\u003ewait_for_device(kVid, kPid, 0, kPollIntervalMs));\n\tEXPECT_FALSE(device_-\u003ewait_for_device(kVid, kPid, -1, kPollIntervalMs));\n}\n```\n\n* **Line 202 (`ReleaseClosesBackend`)**: Use `EXPECT_CALL(*backend_, close()).Times(2);` (1 from `device_-\u003erelease()` + 1 from `~RtsUsbDevice()`) so the assertion is not satisfied by the destructor alone.\n\n[🔗](http://go/tj/594e9a3e-3b69-40fd-bca9-90